## PCM output through the Raspberry Pi's headphone jack: the PWM block fed by a
## DMA ring in the audio driver's memory (`doc/agentic/audio-design.md`).
##
## The samples travel over the io-channel in transmit mode: the client puts
## packets of full buffers of the channel's data pages on it, each buffer
## signed 16-bit stereo frames, left then right, little-endian, at the rate
## `rate` reports; every record a whole number of 4-byte frames at a 4-byte
## aligned address. While the output is open the driver takes the packets in
## the order they were put, one period of the ring at a time, converts their
## frames into the PWM's FIFO words, and hands each packet back on the
## channel, advancing its EventCount, once it copied the packet's last frame
## into the ring: a packet back is a buffer free to refill. The driver looks
## at the channel once per period on its own, so the client never rings it.
## A period that finds too few frames queued is filled up with silence and
## counted as an underrun; the output keeps running. A packet with a record
## outside the data pages or not a whole number of aligned frames comes back
## at once, unplayed. A packet comes back with each record's length cut to
## the bytes the driver took: whole, except for what `close` cuts off.
## Prototype: edited in place.
local interface audio_v1
{
    ## The io-channel the samples travel on: a control page and four data
    ## pages (4096 frames).
    channel 4;

    ## Every function receives the badge of the key it was called through: the
    ## driver tells clients, and their io-channels, apart by it.
    badged;

    ## Another client has the output open, or it is still closing.
    exception busy {}

    ## The caller does not have the output open.
    exception not_open {}

    ## The output's frame rate in millihertz: 44 091 710 (the 100 MHz PWM clock
    ## over a range of 2268 clock cycles per sample).
    rate()
        returns (card32 millihertz);

    ## Open the output for the caller: the driver ramps it from off up to
    ## silence over one period (about 12 ms), then plays the caller's packets
    ## from its io-channel. Packets put before the call play right after the
    ## ramp. Resets the underrun count.
    open()
        raises (busy);

    ## Close the caller's output once what it queued has played: the driver
    ## takes the queued packets as before, without counting the end of them as
    ## an underrun, then ramps from the last frame down to off over one period
    ## and stops. It plays out at most the frames the data pages hold (eight
    ## periods); packets a client keeps putting after that come back cut to
    ## the bytes taken. Returns when the output is off and every packet is
    ## back.
    close()
        raises (not_open);

    ## Periods since the last `open` that found too few frames queued and were
    ## filled up with silence; counted from the first packet after `open`
    ## until `close`.
    underruns()
        returns (card32 periods);
}
